course: everyday
lesson: 395
title: What everyone learned about fights
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: At the end of the summer, Sam walked past the corner by the bus shelter and only noticed halfway that his stomach hadn't gone tight. He hadn't won a fight — he had never had one. What changed was that he had told people, walked with friends, and found out that the boys at the corner were just bored teenagers waiting for a bus. Fear shrinks when you keep facing it with help.
explain: Jake went a whole term without a single fight, which his mum celebrated with a cake that said «0» on it in blue icing. Maddie became captain of the girls' football team, where her toughness was finally useful, and she made a team rule: «we tackle the ball, not people». Connor, who once punched her nose «for equality», said sorry properly in the spring; now they argue about football instead. And Lily's ponytail was left alone by everyone, because the boys had worked out that tugging it made her think less of them, not more.
explain: Kieran joined the cadets, where he learned rules, teamwork and how to march in a straight line, which he claims is harder than it looks. His uncle came to watch the passing-out parade and was so proud that he forgot all about «if you don't hit back, you'll be a target forever». In class, Mrs Okafor asked everyone to write «what I learned about fights». Jake wrote: «Fights are short, but what comes after is long.» Sam wrote: «Being scared is normal. Telling someone is brave.»
explain: The two streets finished the summer with one last football match and a barbecue, where the community officer burned all the sausages and blamed the wind. Afterwards, Sam and Jake walked home together, the long way round, not because they were scared but because they were talking about a comic. Nobody won the war between the streets. It simply stopped, because enough people chose to stop it.
fact: Fear of a place or of people often shrinks when you face it with support from friends and adults.
fact: Courage is not only fighting; telling, walking away, apologising and being kind are also brave.
fact: Fights end quickly, but their consequences, like injuries, punishments and lost trust, last much longer.
fact: Someone who fights a lot can change, especially with help, clear rules and something good to do instead.
fact: Clubs and teams, like football or cadets, give energy and toughness a useful, safe place to go.
fact: A real apology can mend a friendship, even after someone has been hurt.
fact: Teasing or tugging someone to get their attention usually makes them like you less, not more.
fact: Writing down what you learned helps you remember it and understand it better.
fact: A long quarrel between groups usually ends because many people choose to stop, not because one side wins.
fact: People who used to be enemies can become friends.
exercise: What did Sam notice at the corner at the end of the summer? | His stomach did not go tight any more | He was much bigger than the boys there now | The bus shelter had been knocked down | He had won a fight there
exercise: What helped Sam stop being scared of the corner? | Telling people and walking with friends | Winning a fight at the corner in front of everyone | Never going near it again | Growing taller over the summer
exercise: Who were the boys at the corner, really? | Bored teenagers waiting for a bus | A dangerous gang from the next town | Police officers in disguise, watching | Jake's older brothers and their mates
exercise: What did Jake's mum's cake say? | «0», for zero fights all term | «Winner», for winning every fight he had | «Toughest boy» | «Sorry»
exercise: What was Maddie's team rule? | We tackle the ball, not people | Hit back hard if they foul you first | Girls never lose | Only the captain can shout
exercise: Why was football captain a good job for Maddie? | Her toughness was useful there in a safe way | She could fight other teams if they started on her | She never had to run | It meant she could boss Connor around
exercise: How did Connor and Maddie become friends again? | Connor said sorry properly in the spring | Maddie punched him back, harder, in the spring | Their mums made them | They never spoke again
exercise: Why did the boys stop tugging Lily's ponytail? | They realised it made her like them less | Lily cut her hair short | A teacher stood right behind her all day long | Lily tugged their hair back
exercise: What did Kieran learn in the cadets? | Rules, teamwork and marching | How to win street fights properly | How to get out of doing homework | How to be in charge of everyone
exercise: What did Kieran's uncle forget at the parade? | His old «target forever» advice | His camera and his old winter coat | Kieran's name and address | Where the cadets met
exercise: What did Jake write about fights? | «Fights are short, but what comes after is long» | «Fights are the best way to win respect» | «Never tell anyone anything» | «Only big kids should fight»
exercise: What did Sam write? | «Being scared is normal. Telling someone is brave» | «Scared people should just stay at home, safe» | «I should have hit back when I had the chance» | «Brave means never being scared»
exercise: Why did Sam and Jake walk the long way home? | They were busy talking about a comic | They were scared of the corner and the teenagers | They were lost | They wanted to avoid Maddie
exercise: How did the war between the streets end? | Enough people chose to stop it | One street finally won the last big fight | The council closed the rec for good | The adults forced it to end at last
exercise: What did the officer blame for the burnt sausages? | The wind | The children | The barbecue | Maddie's team
exercise: Which of these was a kind of courage this summer? | Saying a real sorry first | Starting a fight to look tough | Laughing loudly at a fight | Keeping quiet about being hurt
exercise: Jake was once the boy who started fights. What does his story show? | People who fight a lot can change with help | Fighters never change, whatever happens | Only small children can change | Changing means becoming a teacher like Mr Pryce
test: Freddie: «so who won the street war in the end?» | Nobody won; it stopped because people chose to stop | Station Road won, because they had more kids | Maddie's street won in the end | The police won
test: «Jake? the fighter? walked home with Sam?? no way». What's the lesson? | Old enemies can become friends | Jake was tricking Sam | Sam had to pay him | It's a mistake, they hate each other
test: «I'm still a bit scared of big boys. am I a wimp?» | No, being scared is normal; telling someone is brave | Yes, until you fight one and see they're not scary | Yes, real kids are never scared | Only if you cry
test: Jake writes «fights are short, what comes after is long». What does he mean? | Bruises, punishments and lost trust last much longer | Fights should be longer | After a fight you always get a long break to calm down | Fights take ages to plan
test: little cousin: «if u tug a girl's hair she'll like u». What did the boys find out? | It makes her like you less | It always works | Only on Fridays | Only if you say sorry after
test: Kieran's uncle used to say «hit back or be a target». What changed his mind? | Seeing Kieran do well with rules and teamwork | Kieran beating someone up | A strong letter from the police to his family | Losing a fight himself
test: Maddie, captain: «we tackle the ball, not people». Why's that a good rule? | It keeps the game tough but safe | It means nobody is allowed to tackle anyone at all | It lets her team win every single match | It's just there to annoy the boys
test: Connor: «sorry I punched you, it wasn't equality, it was just mean». What's this? | A real apology that names the wrong | A joke that doesn't really count for anything | A way to start another fight | Something only teachers say
test: teacher: «what's one kind of courage that isn't fists?» Best answer? | Walking away and telling an adult | Hitting someone bigger than you are | Shouting the loudest in the playground | Never saying sorry
test: a new kid moves in: «which street's the hard one?» Best answer now? | «Neither, we play football together now» | «Ours, we beat them all and kept it for good» | «Station Road, stay away» | «Pick one and fight for it»
test: «I'm too rough for school». What did Maddie and Kieran find instead? | A team or club where toughness is useful and safe | A gang that likes rough kids and wants more | A way to get out of school | Nothing, because rough kids can't ever change
test: Sam to Jake on the walk: «remember when you wanted to fight me?» Jake laughs. What does that show? | Things that felt huge can change completely | Jake still wants to fight them | Sam is being rude | They were never really enemies, they were only pretending
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a pupil's fear of a particular location diminished over the course of a summer. | The pupil confronted the fear repeatedly with the support of others | The pupil prevailed in a fight at that location | The location was permanently closed | The pupil grew in stature sufficiently to deter others from approaching
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequences of a physical altercation? | The altercation is brief, whereas its consequences are of long duration | The consequences cease when the altercation ends | The altercation and its consequences are of equal duration | An altercation carries no consequence whatsoever where no visible injury results
exam: It is given that two neighbouring communities ceased hostilities without either prevailing. Select the explanation consistent with the established facts. | A sufficient number of persons on each side elected to cease | One community surrendered the recreation ground entirely to the other | The authorities compelled a permanent separation | The hostilities ended upon the defeat of one side
exam: State the conclusion most consistent with the observed conduct of a pupil formerly given to frequent fighting. | Persistent combative conduct may be altered with assistance and constructive occupation | Such conduct is fixed and incapable of alteration | Such conduct alters solely through punishment | Such conduct alters only upon departure from school
